  "vulnerability_information": "`cleanarg()` operates on `char *str`, which is the UTF-8 copy allocated by `convert_tchar_to_UTF8()`. It has no reference to the original `wchar_t argv[]` element.\n```c\n/* tool_getparam.c:632 */\nstatic void cleanarg(char *str)\n{\n  /* now that getstr has copied the contents of nextarg, wipe the next\n   * argument out so that the username:password is not displayed in the\n   * system process list */\n  if(str) {\n    size_t len = strlen(str);\n    memset(str, '*', len);\n  }\n}\n```\nIn `parse_args()` (line 3115), the conversion and scrub flow is:\n\n```c\norig_opt = convert_tchar_to_UTF8(argv[i]);      /* UTF-8 copy of the option */\nnextarg  = convert_tchar_to_UTF8(argv[i + 1]);  /* UTF-8 copy of the value  */\nerr = getparameter(orig_opt, nextarg, ...);\n/* inside getparameter, line 3090: */\nif(a->desc & ARG_CLEAR)\n    cleanarg(CURL_UNCONST(nextarg));             /* scrubs the UTF-8 copy    */\nunicodefree(CURL_UNCONST(nextarg));              /* frees the UTF-8 copy     */\n/* argv[i+1] — the original wchar_t pointer — is never touched */\n```\n\nThe original `wchar_t argv[i + 1]` buffer, which is part of the process command line visible through the PEB, is never overwritten by `cleanarg()` or any other routine. `unicodefree()` only frees the converted copy, not the original.\n\n## Impact\n\nA co-located process or user with permission to enumerate process command lines can read credentials supplied via `--user`, `--proxy-user`, `--pass`, `--oauth2-bearer`, `--proxy-pass`, `--tlspassword`, `--proxy-tlspassword`, `--cert`, `--proxy-cert`, `--httpsig-key`, or `--tlsuser` for the entire duration of the curl transfer. This defeats the explicit security goal of `cleanarg()`, which curl's own source comments describe as hiding \"username:password\" from \"the system process list.\"\n\nThe attack requires local access (AV:L) with low privilege (PR:L) — any authenticated Windows user can call `wmic process get commandline` or `Get-CimInstance Win32_Process`. No user interaction is needed (UI:N) and the impact is high confidentiality loss (C:H) of the exposed credentials.",
